.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "gem", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 1. ActiveInteraction
  :markdown

    One solution we really like is
    [ActiveInteraction](https://github.com/AaronLasseigne/active_interaction).
    It is very stable, has wonderful documentation, and gives you a clean way
    to build service objects with support for things like composed
    interactions and even ActiveModel validations.

    Add the gem to your `Gemfile` and run `bundle install`:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # Gemfile
      gem "active_interaction", "~> 5.3"

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "base", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 2. A Base Interaction
  :markdown

    Create a base `ApplicationInteraction` class that all of your service
    objects can inherit from. This gives you a single place to add shared
    behavior later:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/interactions/application_interaction.rb
      class ApplicationInteraction < ActiveInteraction::Base
        # Your interactions will inherit from this class!
      end

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "write", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 3. Writing an Interaction
  :markdown

    Each interaction declares its inputs with typed filters, runs validations,
    and implements an `execute` method. Here is a small example:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/interactions/users/sign_up.rb
      module Users
        class SignUp < ApplicationInteraction
          string :email
          string :name

          validates :email, presence: true

          def execute
            User.create!(email: email, name: name)
          end
        end
      end

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    Every interaction can be called two ways. `.run` always returns an
    **outcome** — check `valid?`, then read `result` or `errors`. `.run!`
    returns the result directly and **raises** `ActiveInteraction::InvalidInteractionError`
    when validation fails:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      outcome = Users::SignUp.run(email: "ada@example.com", name: "Ada")

      if outcome.valid?
        user = outcome.result
      else
        # outcome.errors holds the validation messages
      end

      # Or, where an exception is the failure mode you want:
      user = Users::SignUp.run!(email: "ada@example.com", name: "Ada")

.mt-12.prose
  %h2{ id: "controllers", class: "scroll-mt-24" } 4. Calling It From a Controller
  :markdown
    Which one you reach for is decided by **who sees the failure**. In a
    controller, a validation failure is user feedback — the email was taken,
    the date is in the past — so it belongs in a flash message next to a
    redirect or re-render, never in an exception. Use `.run`, check the
    outcome, and surface the errors:

.mt-6.max-w-prose
  = doc_code(language: "ruby") do
    :plain
      # app/controllers/users_controller.rb
      def create
        outcome = Users::SignUp.run(sign_up_params)
        return redirect_to new_user_path, alert: outcome.errors.full_messages.to_sentence unless outcome.valid?

        redirect_to outcome.result, notice: "Welcome aboard!"
      end

= doc_note(css: "mt-6", modifier: :warning, title: "Never run! in a Controller") do
  :markdown
    `.run!` turns a validation failure into an unhandled exception — a raw
    **500** page for the user and an error report for you — for something
    that was never a bug. The outcome pattern above costs one guard clause
    and gives the user a message they can act on.

.mt-6.prose
  :markdown
    `.run!` is the right call **outside** the request cycle, wherever an
    exception is the correct failure mode and something upstream is prepared
    to handle it:

    - **Background jobs** — let the exception fail the job so the queue's
      retry and dead-letter semantics take over.
    - **Seeds and rake tasks** — a bad record should halt the run loudly, not
      leave a half-seeded database behind a swallowed outcome.
    - **Tests** — `run!` gives you the result in one line and turns an
      unexpected validation failure into a failing example with a real
      message.

= doc_note(css: "mt-8", modifier: :tip, title: "Pro Tip") do
  :markdown
    Because interactions support **composed** interactions, you can call one
    service from another with `compose`, building larger workflows out of
    small, well-tested pieces.

